1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:04,037
Can this Apollo fifteen reflector track lunar recession?

2
00:00:03,987 --> 00:00:06,487
A beam returns from the Moon.

3
00:00:06,487 --> 00:00:11,250
How does that return yield thirty eight point three zero millimetres per year?

4
00:00:11,250 --> 00:00:16,275
Four ledger columns: measured, dated, counted, assumed.

5
00:00:16,275 --> 00:00:20,537
Laser timing is observed; the recession rate is inferred.

6
00:00:20,537 --> 00:00:26,087
Calculated: zero point zero eight divided by thirty eight point three zero.

7
00:00:26,087 --> 00:00:28,600
What controls that uncertainty?

8
00:00:28,600 --> 00:00:31,212
Station one: the count.

9
00:00:31,212 --> 00:00:36,100
Lunar laser ranging begins with emitted pulses and returned photons.

10
00:00:36,100 --> 00:00:41,775
A station records timing, not a ruler stretching from Earth to a fixed mark on the Moon.

11
00:00:41,775 --> 00:00:47,775
The Moon moves throughout the experiment, so even the idea of a static distance would mislead us.

12
00:00:47,775 --> 00:00:53,637
The International Laser Ranging Service describes full-rate observations and normal points.

13
00:00:53,637 --> 00:00:58,475
A normal point condenses observations collected over seconds or minutes.

14
00:00:58,475 --> 00:01:07,962
These are different records: one retains more of the observation detail, while the other is a compact product for scientific analysis and further comparison.

15
00:01:07,962 --> 00:01:13,975
Apollo missions and the Soviet Lunokhod missions placed retroreflectors on the lunar surface.

16
00:01:13,975 --> 00:01:18,500
A retroreflector sends an incoming signal back toward its source.

17
00:01:18,500 --> 00:01:26,887
The route is out and back, and the observing station must identify the small useful return amid all other signals arriving at its detector.

18
00:01:26,887 --> 00:01:34,062
In our simplified diagram, distance equals light speed multiplied by round-trip time, divided by two.

19
00:01:34,062 --> 00:01:38,475
This is our teaching sketch, not the full lunar-ranging solution.

20
00:01:38,475 --> 00:01:48,112
Atmospheric corrections, station location, reflector geometry and orbital motion complicate the path from one observed pulse to a useful parameter.

21
00:01:48,112 --> 00:01:55,700
The service says stations apply corrections for atmospheric conditions and ground calibration before releasing laser ranges.

22
00:01:55,700 --> 00:02:01,512
This matters because a published range must not be confused with an untouched stopwatch reading.

23
00:02:01,512 --> 00:02:09,925
The archive holds prepared scientific observations, with documented formats, not simply a list of uncorrected travel times.

24
00:02:09,925 --> 00:02:16,825
Notice what has not appeared in this notebook: no millimetres-per-year trend has been measured directly by one pulse.

25
00:02:16,825 --> 00:02:19,925
A pulse contributes one observation.

26
00:02:19,925 --> 00:02:25,012
Many observations across changing geometry constrain a dynamical model.

27
00:02:25,012 --> 00:02:29,725
Confusing the two would exaggerate what the apparatus actually records.

28
00:02:29,725 --> 00:02:35,325
At the first station, our ledger reads measured: timing and calibrated ranges.

29
00:02:35,325 --> 00:02:38,125
Dated: the observing epochs.

30
00:02:38,125 --> 00:02:43,025
Counted: observation records, but without an invented total here.

31
00:02:43,025 --> 00:02:47,112
Assumed: a model of motion and fitted parameters.

32
00:02:47,112 --> 00:02:52,237
The columns answer different questions, and none can silently replace another.

33
00:02:52,237 --> 00:02:54,937
Station two: the sum.

34
00:02:54,937 --> 00:03:02,587
Williams and Boggs published an analysis of secular tidal changes in lunar orbit and Earth rotation in twenty sixteen.

35
00:03:02,587 --> 00:03:11,900
The abstract reports a lunar semimajor-axis rate of thirty eight point three zero, plus or minus zero point zero eight, millimetres per year.

36
00:03:11,900 --> 00:03:15,787
The semimajor axis describes the scale of an orbit.

37
00:03:15,787 --> 00:03:22,262
It is not identical to the range between a particular ground station and a particular reflector on one night.

38
00:03:22,262 --> 00:03:30,262
The measured geometric range changes with Earth's rotation, orbital phase and the positions of the observing and reflecting sites.

39
00:03:30,262 --> 00:03:36,500
A teaching ledger might write observed range equals modelled geometry plus corrections and residual.

40
00:03:36,500 --> 00:03:41,350
That is our bookkeeping illustration, not a quoted equation from the paper.

41
00:03:41,350 --> 00:03:49,887
Fitting parameters across observations helps explain a pattern of residuals; it does not turn each residual into a separate measurement of recession.

42
00:03:49,887 --> 00:03:57,462
The authors analyse lunar laser ranging data together with parameters related to terrestrial and lunar tidal dissipation.

43
00:03:57,462 --> 00:04:02,012
Converting estimated dissipation parameters gives secular rates.

44
00:04:02,012 --> 00:04:09,000
This is the essential missing bridge between timing photons and putting millimetres per year beside an orbital quantity.

45
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:16,462
Write thirty eight point three zero on the sum sheet, with zero point zero eight beside it as the reported uncertainty.

46
00:04:16,462 --> 00:04:24,800
Dividing zero point zero eight by thirty eight point three zero gives approximately zero point two one per cent, calculated.

47
00:04:24,800 --> 00:04:29,950
That ratio is descriptive, not the uncertainty of an individual pulse.

48
00:04:30,100 --> 00:04:33,100
Sensitivity is visible on paper.

49
00:04:33,050 --> 00:04:40,825
Thirty eight point two two and thirty eight point three eight lie one quoted uncertainty unit on either side of the central estimate.

50
00:04:40,825 --> 00:04:43,762
They illustrate that numerical interval.

51
00:04:43,762 --> 00:04:48,662
They do not guarantee that every unmodelled systematic effect lies within it.

52
00:04:48,662 --> 00:05:00,687
Here we can close the opening question carefully: the plus-or-minus value belongs to a reported parameter estimate under its analysis, not to a laser's single-shot resolution or a photographed beam width.

53
00:05:00,687 --> 00:05:06,887
Observations, corrections and modelling assumptions are what link the measured ranges to that result.

54
00:05:06,887 --> 00:05:19,212
Williams, Turyshev and Boggs reported thirty eight point zero eight, plus or minus zero point one nine, millimetres per year in twenty fourteen, using forty three years of lunar ranging.

55
00:05:19,212 --> 00:05:21,450
That was a different study.

56
00:05:21,450 --> 00:05:26,850
We must not blend its number with the later estimate or claim a direct instrument comparison.

57
00:05:26,850 --> 00:05:29,987
Station three: outside the sum.

58
00:05:29,987 --> 00:05:34,925
A picture of a reflector verifies the apparatus to which signals return.

59
00:05:34,925 --> 00:05:38,600
It does not itself supply the published recession rate.

60
00:05:38,600 --> 00:05:41,700
Neither does a single dated observation.

61
00:05:41,700 --> 00:05:46,750
Both can be valid evidence without being the fitted result of the full analysis.

62
00:05:46,750 --> 00:05:52,700
NASA describes newer lunar retroreflectors as targets for Earth-based laser pulses.

63
00:05:52,700 --> 00:05:56,900
Measuring the round-trip transit time helps determine distance.

64
00:05:56,900 --> 00:06:01,475
This institutional instrument description establishes the physical method.

65
00:06:01,475 --> 00:06:08,500
It cannot independently validate the exact uncertainty stated in a distinct twenty sixteen orbital analysis.

66
00:06:08,500 --> 00:06:13,112
The laser archive contains records researchers can reanalyse.

67
00:06:13,112 --> 00:06:22,737
The International Laser Ranging Service lists older and newer observation formats and reflector targets including Apollo eleven, fourteen and fifteen.

68
00:06:22,737 --> 00:06:30,612
These records are evidence of observed ranges, not automatic proof that any particular fitted tidal model is uniquely correct.

69
00:06:30,612 --> 00:06:35,550
Williams and Boggs also report a rate of change for the Moon's mean motion.

70
00:06:35,550 --> 00:06:40,662
That is another dynamical-fit output, not a second ruler buried in the Moon.

71
00:06:40,662 --> 00:06:47,550
We keep it outside our central sum, because related model outputs are not automatically independent confirmation.

72
00:06:47,550 --> 00:06:51,750
In another notebook, ancient coral growth markings are counted.

73
00:06:51,750 --> 00:06:55,175
In this notebook, returning light is timed.

74
00:06:55,175 --> 00:06:58,987
The two records occupy different evidential columns.

75
00:06:58,987 --> 00:07:06,512
The coral count does not become an input to the laser model just because both subjects appear in accounts of Earth's evolving rotation.

76
00:07:06,512 --> 00:07:10,462
There is a limit to what we can claim from the published abstract.

77
00:07:10,462 --> 00:07:21,075
It does not provide the full covariance matrix or a detailed numerical breakdown assigning parts of zero point zero eight to particular instruments, observations or corrections.

78
00:07:21,075 --> 00:07:24,775
Giving such a breakdown here would be invented precision.

79
00:07:24,775 --> 00:07:31,962
And a present-day fitted rate cannot simply be multiplied by millions of years to reconstruct past lunar distances.

80
00:07:31,962 --> 00:07:36,562
The cited research discusses the evolution of tidal dissipation.

81
00:07:36,562 --> 00:07:43,975
A constant-rate extrapolation would add an unverified assumption and hide changes that the physical model is intended to consider.

82
00:07:43,975 --> 00:07:46,387
Return to four columns.

83
00:07:46,387 --> 00:07:50,175
Measured means ranges obtained from timed pulses.

84
00:07:50,175 --> 00:07:53,037
Dated means observation epochs.

85
00:07:53,037 --> 00:07:58,062
Counted means records and their grouping, without a fabricated sample count.

86
00:07:58,062 --> 00:08:02,325
Assumed means fitted dynamical models and their conditions.

87
00:08:02,325 --> 00:08:05,725
Each column must retain its name on the page.

88
00:08:05,725 --> 00:08:08,525
Entry one: the apparatus.

89
00:08:08,525 --> 00:08:12,675
A retroreflector permits a round-trip timing experiment.

90
00:08:12,675 --> 00:08:15,075
Record type: measured.

91
00:08:15,075 --> 00:08:20,250
Sources: the international ranging archive and NASA's instrument account.

92
00:08:20,250 --> 00:08:27,587
No rate of recession belongs in this row, because a physical timing observation and a fitted trend are different things.

93
00:08:27,587 --> 00:08:36,725
Entry two: the twenty sixteen published result, thirty eight point three zero plus or minus zero point zero eight millimetres per year.

94
00:08:36,725 --> 00:08:39,862
Record type: derived parameter.

95
00:08:39,862 --> 00:08:48,362
This precision is a claim of Williams and Boggs' analysis, not a property shared by every reflector, station or laser pulse.

96
00:08:48,532 --> 00:08:55,282
Entry three: our ratio, about zero point two one per cent. Record type: calculated.

97
00:08:55,232 --> 00:09:00,119
The numerator and denominator stay visible so the arithmetic can be checked.

98
00:09:00,119 --> 00:09:06,407
If a future analysis revises either published value, this derived ratio changes as well.

99
00:09:06,407 --> 00:09:09,469
It is not new observational evidence.

100
00:09:09,469 --> 00:09:13,069
Entry four: what remains unknown here.

101
00:09:13,069 --> 00:09:19,994
An abstract can authenticate a reported number; it does not reveal every data-selection or covariance decision.

102
00:09:19,994 --> 00:09:24,444
We leave the detailed uncertainty budget empty rather than making it up.

103
00:09:24,444 --> 00:09:28,169
That blank is part of the scientific record's honesty.

104
00:09:28,169 --> 00:09:37,694
Three things could change the inferred result: an extended span of observations, revised range corrections, or altered dynamical assumptions.

105
00:09:37,694 --> 00:09:41,057
This does not predict the direction of any change.

106
00:09:41,057 --> 00:09:47,069
It tells us what sorts of evidence and decisions must be checked before comparing two fitted answers.

107
00:09:47,069 --> 00:09:49,307
Return to the reflector.

108
00:09:49,307 --> 00:09:53,757
We started with returning light and ended with an estimated orbital rate.

109
00:09:53,757 --> 00:09:59,019
Between them sit dated observations, a model, and reported uncertainty.

110
00:09:59,019 --> 00:10:05,594
A careful illustration keeps those separate even when a graphic compresses the story into one striking figure.

111
00:10:05,594 --> 00:10:10,057
A laser pulse gives us a timed return, not a recession rate.

112
00:10:10,057 --> 00:10:16,832
That rate comes from fitting many dated observations to an orbital model, with uncertainty attached to the estimate.

113
00:10:16,832 --> 00:10:20,057
The evidence ledger makes that difference visible.

